\description{
Find PRIDE Archive projects by keyword, with every page fetched and no accession repeated: the discovery step that produces the accessions every other pride verb takes.

\strong{The discovery entry point.} Every other \code{pride_} function takes an accession you already
have; this is the one that produces them, so you can go from a subject to a dataset without
leaving R. Paging is handled for you: however many pages the result set spans, you get one
data.frame, with no accession repeated.
}
\value{
A data.frame with one row per matching project, in PRIDE's ranking order. \strong{Zero rows
  is a real answer} - PRIDE reports no hits as an empty result, and unlike \code{\link{pride_list_files}}
  this does not raise \code{mzlib_project_not_found}, because no accession here could have been a
  typo.

  Text columns: \code{accession}, \code{title}, \code{project_description}, \code{sample_processing_protocol},
  \code{data_processing_protocol}, \code{doi}, \code{submission_type} and \code{sdrf}. Dates: \code{submission_date},
  \code{publication_date} and \code{updated_date}, as \code{Date} (bare calendar dates, never timestamps), \code{NA}
  when PRIDE reported none. Popularity: \code{download_count} in downloads, \code{avg_downloads_per_file}
  in downloads per file, \code{percentile} a download-popularity percentile within PRIDE, and
  \code{bot_count}, \code{hub_count} and \code{organic_count} in downloads split by traffic kind. List
  columns, one character vector per hit: \code{project_tags}, \code{keywords}, \code{submitters}, \code{lab_pis},
  \code{affiliations}, \code{instruments}, \code{softwares}, \code{quantification_methods}, \code{sample_attributes},
  \code{organisms}, \code{organism_parts}, \code{diseases}, \code{references}, \code{experiment_types},
  \code{project_file_names} and \code{other_omics_links}; \code{highlights}, a named list per hit (PRIDE field
  to matched snippets, the terms wrapped in \code{<em>}); \code{matched_fields}, its sorted names; and
  \code{yearly_downloads}, a data.frame per hit of \code{year} and \code{count}.

}
\section{A hit is not a project's metadata}{


PRIDE serves search from a separate index in which every controlled-vocabulary field is
\strong{flattened to a display string}: instruments arrive as \code{"Q Exactive"} with no accession,
contacts as names, publications as pre-formatted citation strings, and \code{sdrf} as one
space-joined bag of term values - not a file, name or URL. Follow the \code{accession} for the
vocabulary. \code{project_file_names} is not the manifest either: use \code{\link{pride_list_files}} or
\code{\link{pride_list_ftp_files}} to act on files.

}
\section{Zero and empty mean not reported}{


PRIDE sends no nulls, so an absent count arrives as \strong{0} and an absent list as empty. A
\code{download_count} of 0 means "not reported", never "nobody downloaded it", and several fields are
genuinely sparse: sampled over 1,600 hits, \code{project_tags} was populated on 2.6\%, \code{sdrf} on 2.4\%,
and the bot/hub/organic counts on under half. \code{keywords} may hold empty and whitespace-only
strings, about 9\% of hits; they are passed through, so filter before joining.

}
\section{A live index}{


PRIDE pages a live index with no stable cursor. A project published mid-fetch is deduplicated,
but one removed mid-fetch can fall between two pages and be missed. A result set that fits on
one page cannot be affected.

}
